 "narrative": "The first analysis round provides two very strong dataset associations and one very strong reversal. Because estimates are already signed to the registered direction, positive estimates support the claim and negative estimates contradict it. H6 (SMARCA4 loss versus SMARCA2 dependency) has a signed difference of +0.784, 95% CI [0.765,0.803]; given the very negative CRISPR score means stronger dependency, this corresponds to a huge raw decrease in SMARCA2 dependency in SMARCA4-loss cells. The magnitude is far above the SD of SMARCA2 scores and is clinically important under a 10% relative-change standard. H7 (MSI-high versus WRN dependency) is similarly strong: +0.790, CI [0.760,0.819], supporting stronger WRN dependency in MSI-high cells. These effects are so large that multiplicity, cell-count adequacy, and baseline-scale concerns do not materially threaten their interpretation as associations in this dataset; they are still not causal claims.\n\nH8 is the clearest opposite signal. For KEAP1 loss versus NFE2L2 dependency, the registered expectation was stronger NFE2L2 dependency (direction -1), but the signed estimate is -0.796 with CI [-0.816,-0.776]. Since direction is -1, this means the raw contrast is strongly positive: KEAP1-loss cells have weaker or positive NFE2L2 dependency. Thus H8 is rejected as stated, but the finding justifies a registered reversal for later formal evaluation.\n\nH3 is borderline but biologically and clinically interesting: EGFR amplification versus EGFR dependency has a positive signed estimate of +0.025, CI [-0.00025,0.051]. If the comparator mean is near the overall mean around -0.25, the point estimate is roughly a 10% stronger dependency, and the upper bound is well above 10%, while the lower bound just crosses zero. This is not sufficient to accept now, so I request independent validation of H3 because it is the claim where an independent interval is most likely to change the conclusion.\n\nThe remaining null or near-null results are mostly low priority. H1 is imprecise but small: the expected KRAS mutation effect on KRAS dependency is +0.014 signed, CI [-0.004,0.031]. It remains unresolved and active because KRAS co-mutation contexts may modify the effect. H2 rejects the specific hypothesis that BRAF V600E strengthens BRAF dependency: the upper end of the signed interval is only +0.012, which is not clinically meaningful on the BRFScore scale, and the point estimate is near zero. H4, H5, and H9 remain unresolved/deferred because their intervals are wide or centered near zero; they are not compelling enough for immediate follow-up, but I do not close them as definitive nulls. H10, H11, and H12 are rejected as meaningful directional claims: the intervals are near zero, and the clinically meaningful portion of the intervals is either absent or in the opposite direction (for H12, the expected stronger STAT3 dependency is essentially excluded). I am treating research marker/signature variables cautiously; they are not used as biological evidence here because their assigned roles are absent, and I am not claiming causality or generalizability from these associations alone.",
 "research_notes": "Round-1 conclusions in this dataset: accept SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency and MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency. Reject original KEAP1 loss -> stronger NFE2L2 dependency and propose reversal H8. Keep EGFR amplification -> EGFR dependency unresolved and request validation H3. Low-priority nulls: ERBB2 amplification, MYC amplification for USP7/DHX9/POLR2A, STAT3/EGFR. Next priorities: (1) assess H3 independent validation; (2) test reversals/refinements (KEAP1 opposite direction); (3) test context-specificity of accepted strong effects, especially SMARCA2/SMARCA4 and WRN/MSI in adenocarcinoma; (4) explore remaining outcomes with plausible binary predictors (RPL11 with MYC amplification, TMED10 with EGFR amplification, RIT1 with KRAS mutation). Avoid overinterpreting near-zero outcomes by relative percent alone; use absolute score differences and standard deviations.",
